    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In recognition of the seismic hazard along the North Anatolian fault within the Marmara Sea region, geophysical surveys over the past six years have provided high-resolution multi-beam bathymetry and a variety of seismic reflection data sets with variable resolution and depth of penetration. Despite the increased quantity of data, the fault geometry within the Marmara Sea remains in debate. As does the issue of which faults within that geometry may be considered active and susceptible to a future earthquake. In this paper I present the Marmara Sea seismic reflection and bathymetry data as well as preliminary results demonstrating the ability for three-dimensional mechanical modeling to quantitatively evaluate various proposed fault geometries. Specifically, I show that the fault geometry in the Tekirda􏲮 basin region requires lateral bounding faults as well as the large east-west trending faults to the north and south to produce the basin geometry observed in the seismic and bathymetry data. This predicted geometry is in contrast to the simpler single fault hypothesis put forth recently by several authors.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
